WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.980
So, some two years ago I was having the
very first coaching lesson with a

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.150
student of mine, who had been trying to
learn Spanish but to no avail, and it

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:13.349
turned out that he had been, you know,
learning the language for a few months

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:18.390
and I asked him: "what are you doing
to learn Spanish?",  and it turns out that

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:22.500
he was actually listening to the radio
and listening massively in order to

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.000
understand, in the hope that he would
understand everything with time.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:33.290
And of course listening comprehension
like all good things takes time, it takes

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:39.530
exposure, but you have to learn how to do it. You might be in the same situation

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:42.710
right now maybe you're learning a
language and you're wondering why you

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:46.910
still don't understand the language, why
is it so difficult, why are you

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:52.010
struggling, and in this very same video
I'm going to give you some fundamental

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:58.500
tips on how to make your listening comprehension fantastic in a relatively short amount of time.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.610
So in this video I'm going to give you the

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:05.870
fundamental rules of thumb or tips in
order to understand any language quickly

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:10.700
and effectively and we're going to have
a very cool extra tip at the end,

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:18.320
so please, stay tuned. The fundamental
rule of thumb in order to understand the

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.240
language better and better is to get
exposed to comprehensible input, that is,

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:28.790
input that you can understand and when
you are a beginner when you first start

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:34.940
learning a language, make sure that you
always always listen and read at the

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:38.840
same time because if you can read and
listen at the same time, you will be able

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:44.210
to actually SEE the words that are being
spoken and this allows you to build

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:49.460
comprehensible input that will help you
in the intermediate stage when you can

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:54.440
venture into the unknown ground of pure
listening. But you have to remember that

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:58.460
the listening comprehension process as
well as the language learning process is

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.990
not linear but it's progressive, so
always make sure no matter what stage

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:08.030
you're in that you expose yourself to
comprehensible input, the input that you

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:12.100
can' understand at least to 70 or 80
percent.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:19.459
How many times have you found yourself sitting in a class and not caring at all about when you were

00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:22.040
listening to? This is common especially and when

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.189
to language learning at school at
university because people are not

00:02:25.189 --> 00:02:29.030
interested, they're in a passive state of
mind, and one of the reasons is because

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:32.599
they're not interested in what they're
listening to. They they don't care, they

00:02:32.599 --> 00:02:36.799
didn't choose the material, they didn't
choose the audio or the text. So as an

00:02:36.799 --> 00:02:40.579
independent language learner, YOU have
the possibility of listening to whatever

00:02:40.579 --> 00:02:44.989
you like. Make sure that what you listen
to is something you find interesting

00:02:44.989 --> 00:02:50.599
because if it is interesting for you, your
levels of dopamine in your brain

00:02:50.599 --> 00:02:55.200
will literally skyrocket and you will
absorb the content much better.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.819
Have you ever found yourself interacting with native speakers in a language you still

00:03:01.819 --> 00:03:06.200
didn't know well and you're trying to
understand every single word they try

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.549
that they were saying to you? Well I
think that a better technique or a

00:03:10.549 --> 00:03:13.700
better strategy is to simply focus on
the big picture.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.939
I really like the sentence in English
"missing the forest for the trees" - you

00:03:16.939 --> 00:03:20.959
don't want to "miss the forest for the
trees" so even if you don't understand

00:03:20.959 --> 00:03:25.519
all the words, focus on the big picture
so focus on the cue words inside the

00:03:25.519 --> 00:03:28.699
sentence that would give you the meaning
of the sentence even if you don't

00:03:28.699 --> 00:03:32.449
understand all the elements of the
sentence itself, this is really important

00:03:32.449 --> 00:03:35.930
so relax you need to understand
everything you just focus on the main

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:39.200
things on the main cues, and focus on the
big picture.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:48.060
Every uttered sentence in any language is
a complex combination of sounds.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:53.280
In order to make your life easier  to
understand, absorb and replicate these

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.970
phonetic pattern, you might want to make
your life easier and do things

00:03:56.970 --> 00:04:01.800
step-by-step. One very very easy
strategy in order to tackle this problem

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:07.260
is very simple: you just slow the audio
down. So take the audio track or the

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:11.520
audio tracks that you have, play them at
normal speed and then play them a little

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.450
bit more slowly. In order to do this you
have many possibilities nowadays, for

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:19.350
example you can use free software
programs that you can download from

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:24.330
the internet like AuDIria (or AudiRIa)
but you can also use Google Translate.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:29.880
You can simply put a sentence inside the
box and play it and the second time it

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:36.060
will be uttered slowlier. So this is very
simple: make sure that you always slow

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.950
down the audio, look at the bigger
picture, look at the details, and make it

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:45.000
a habit. Especially at the beginning, this
will make a big difference.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:53.160
if I say the word "listening" associated with language
learning, probably you think

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:58.950
Ok, listening to a podcast or listening and
watching a movie or listening to music

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:03.330
in a foreign language. That is true, but
there are so many other activities you

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:07.950
can engage in when it comes to listening,
and it's not only WHAT you do but HOW

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:11.250
you do it and WHERE you put your
attention. You can for example

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:16.200
listen to a podcast just for the sake of
listening to it while you are running some

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:21.780
errands but you can also listen to a
podcast focusing on the pronunciation

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:26.629
and intonation of the language that you
you're listening to or you can even take

00:05:26.629 --> 00:05:30.169
notes that is you're listening to a
podcast in a foreign language for the

00:05:30.169 --> 00:05:34.639
sake of grabbing and absorbing
information so what you do and how you

00:05:34.639 --> 00:05:39.379
do it counts and you have to make it
varied. The more varied activities the better.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:47.100
"Good things take time" - I always say
it out loud to myself when things get frustrating

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:51.440
and they do get frustrating when for example I don't understand the

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.879
language still, when I would like to talk
to someone and I still just understand

00:05:55.879 --> 00:06:00.169
the gist of it, when I don't understand
the radio. So you have to remind yourself

00:06:00.169 --> 00:06:05.539
that it takes massive exposure to
understand the language, it takes volume

00:06:05.539 --> 00:06:10.729
it takes sampling. These are the most
important nutrients for your brain. So on

00:06:10.729 --> 00:06:14.630
the one hand you need massive exposure
on the other hand you have to find the

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:18.620
system to distribute this massive
exposure you have to do it day by day.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:24.500
That's the best way and how to do that?
Well, just one of the strategy is to

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:29.569
associate your listening activity with
something else for example you just

00:06:29.569 --> 00:06:33.979
listen to something that you can
obviously understand and you do it while

00:06:33.979 --> 00:06:37.370
you're doing something else
I myself do some work out in the morning

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:41.419
while I listen to Polish podcasts. So
remember to do something every single

00:06:41.419 --> 00:06:44.449
day, be consistent, good things take time but

00:06:44.449 --> 00:06:48.000
if you do that, you will make it happen.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.650
So here you have it: listening and
understanding the language takes time

00:06:54.650 --> 00:07:00.500
but it's not difficult you learn how to
do it and you have to be consistent.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:04.490
Now if you want to know more just click on the description box below and head over

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:09.229
my blog where you will be able to listen
to the article I put together and you

00:07:09.229 --> 00:07:13.190
can even just listen to it so you can do
this while doing something

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:19.250
as I suggested in this very same video.
Now I have one question and one extra

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:24.020
tip for you let's start with a question.
What is the biggest struggle that you

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:27.320
have when it comes to listening
comprehension and what have you done to

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:31.730
overcome it or what do you think you can do to overcome it? So since you've been in

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:35.780
so patient I want to reward you for your
patience and I'm going to give you one

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:41.870
cool extra tip and here it is: When we think about listening, we tend to think that

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:47.450
listening is just a passive activity
just listen to something and the sounds

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:52.490
flow into your ear but you can actually
do something that can completely change

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:56.990
your strategy and your listening
comprehension and that is be ACTIVE. Let

00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:00.350
me give you a very simple example when
you listen to something that obviously

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:04.790
you can understand, you can just listen
to it maybe you're running some errands

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:11.480
or you can sit down and take notes when
you take notes you will realize how your

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:16.400
attention changes because your entire
body is listening to that piece of

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:20.660
information. So the next time take a
youtube video, listen to it and take

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:24.380
notes, and you will see how that
influences and improves the way you

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:29.000
understand the piece of content and the
way you absorb it.

